    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. the incision looks a bit red today and there is some clear fluid coming out of it, i'm worried about something nasty developing under the skin. how do i tell if there's an actual infection brewing deep down where i can't see it? also what should the diet look like for the first few days?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Redness and clear fluid can be normal initial signs of healing, but if you're worried about a deep infection, you need to watch for systemic signs like fever, decreased appetite, or general malaise. Deep infections are often only detectable through clinical signs or, more reliably, through veterinary examination or diagnostic tests. For the first few days, the diet should be highly digestible and easily tolerated, often starting with a very bland, highly palatable slurry or liquid feed to keep the gut moving gently.\n\nIt's very important to keep the area clean and dry, which is a good preventative measure against any developing infection. If you see any signs of the infection spreading—like increasing heat, pain, or discharge that changes color or smell—call your vet immediately. They might need to perform a more thorough check, perhaps even sampling the area, to rule out anything serious.",
      "source": "target",
